## Content Quality Standard Established
Discussed extensively: citing sources ≠ scraping. The standard agreed on:
- Every page must stand alone as genuinely useful even if the reader never visits another source
- Add an original layer: data, decision frameworks, real examples, opinions, usable formats (tables/checklists)
- For `nail-costs` specifically: the fix isn't merging pages, it's adding real specificity (real sourced data, expert quotes, dated info, author credentials) so content doesn't read as generic/scraped
- User pushed back correctly on "just claim you called salons" — agreed fabricating primary research is dishonest; real (even small-scale) research or proper citation of real published sources is the honest path

