10. **Nail Anatomy & Basics** (matrix/bed/plate, growth) — Foundation/low direct monetization

Priority order for expert interview coverage: 1 → 4 → 3 → 2 → 7 → 5 → 9 → (6, 8, 10 lower priority)

## Expert Sourcing Strategy Established
Key realization user pushed toward: don't need 1 expert per post (impossible at 1165 posts), need ~1 expert PER CATEGORY, interviewed once in a long-form Q&A, reused across all posts in that category. This is standard industry practice (e.g., Healthline model), not a Google red flag, as long as the expert is real/verifiable and content stays genuinely relevant per post.

**Free sourcing channels identified:** personal network, own audience/followers, local salons (backlink trade), Reddit communities (r/Nailtechs, r/AskNailTechs), LinkedIn outreach, nursing/PA students as lower-credential fallback.

**Images:** Site uses ~5000 AI-generated images (user confirmed, not scraped — no consent/legal issue). Recommendation: add one site-wide AI-image disclosure line; keep AI for Design/Seasonal/Gallery categories; use real photos going forward for Health/How-To/Cost categories where factual/replicable claims are made.

**Instagram/creator outreach for images/reels:** discussed that a casual "sure!" reply is weak legal protection; recommended explicit written terms (specific content named, usage scope, edit rights, "credit only, no payment" stated upfront, they retain ownership) even via informal email.

